            if grid[r][c] != 0 and not seen[r][c]:
                stack = [(r, c)]; seen[r][c] = True; comp = []
                while stack:
                    y, x = stack.pop(); comp.append((y, x))
                    for dy, dx in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):
                        ny, nx = y+dy, x+dx
                        if 0 <= ny < h and 0 <= nx < w and not seen[ny][nx] and grid[ny][nx] != 0:
                            seen[ny][nx] = True; stack.append((ny, nx))
                col = 9 if len(comp) == 1 else 8
                for y, x in comp:
                    ny, nx = y-1, x-2
                    if 0 <= ny < h and 0 <= nx < w:
                        out[ny][nx] = col
    return out
```

Every colored cell slides straight up within its column, keeping its order. After that, any cell with no orthogonal neighbor of the same color is cleared.

I hand-checked this against Example 1 (columns 0–2) and Example 3 (column 9), and those match the expected outputs. I have not traced Example 2 or every column.

```python
def transform(grid: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:
    h = len(grid); w = len(grid[0])
    g = [[0]*w for _ in range(h)]
    for c in range(w):
        vals = [grid[r][c] for r in range(h) if grid[r][c] != 0]
        for i, v in enumerate(vals):
            g[i][c] = v
    out = [row[:] for row in g]
    for r in range(h):
        for c in range(w):
            v = g[r][c]
            if v == 0:
                continue
            ok = False
            for dr, dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):
                rr, cc = r+dr, c+dc
                if 0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w and g[rr][cc] == v:
                    ok = True
                    break
            if not ok:
                out[r][c] = 0
    return out
